\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.1}{\ignorespaces Experimental measurements from a single shot where the probes are at $z=0$. The drive cylinder at $r = 0.6$ m turns on at $t = 0$. This releases the magnetic pressure at high radii allowing the plasma to expand which leads to a pressure wavefront moving radially inwards (orange). Later, a shock (green) is formed in front of the reconnection layer (purple dashed) which moves radially inwards. Once the wavefront passes, the magnetic field lines start moving outwards following the flux contours (black).}}{17}{figure.caption.18}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:experimental-evolution}{{2.1}{17}{Experimental measurements from a single shot where the probes are at $z=0$. The drive cylinder at $r = 0.6$ m turns on at $t = 0$. This releases the magnetic pressure at high radii allowing the plasma to expand which leads to a pressure wavefront moving radially inwards (orange). Later, a shock (green) is formed in front of the reconnection layer (purple dashed) which moves radially inwards. Once the wavefront passes, the magnetic field lines start moving outwards following the flux contours (black)}{figure.caption.18}{}}
